TODO
====

Grouped by kind rather than priority. Line references were checked against the
tree; if one has drifted, the description should still find it.


Finishing the port
------------------

Linux and Windows are done: both build, run, and have been heard. All three
platforms are real CI gates. What is left is macOS on an actual desktop.

- macOS has never been run outside CI. It builds and the harnesses pass, but
  nobody has seen the GUI and no sound has come out of it. Quartz is the
  backend most likely to disagree with the layout.

- Nothing has verified that a package runs on a machine with no GTK at all.
  CI has no such machine, so a package that only works where it was built looks
  identical to a good one. The bundling is exercised; the closure under it is
  not.  (PACKAGING.md)

- cmake/Packaging.cmake:114 still queries gtkmm-3.0 for its prefix, and the
  comment at :36 still says gtkmm-3. The tree is on gtkmm-4, so that lookup
  returns nothing -- which shows up as a missing dependency on a machine you do
  not have. Windows working does not clear this: MSYS2 keeps the whole stack in
  one prefix, so the closure finds the libraries by another route there.


Never heard
-----------

- No MIDI has ever been played through the RtMidi path, on any platform. The
  headless checks pass; that is not the same thing.

- Latency, underrun behaviour under load, and whether RtAudio's JACK path is as
  good as the hand-written one are all open and want an ear. JACK users are the
  audience that will notice.


Defects
-------

- DestroyMap takes its map by value (libthink/think.h:253). Six call sites, so
  unloadPlugins() and five others leave dangling pointers in the caller's map.
  One '&'.

- thMidiController::connections_[16][128] is indexed by raw MIDI bytes with no
  clamping (libthink/thMidiController.h:52).

- gthPatchManager::newPatch indexes patches_[chan] with no bounds check
  (src/gthPatchfile.cpp:91) -- the first thing it does is delete patches_[chan].
  Latent rather than live now the notebook always holds sixteen pages.

- ArgTable binds a raw thArg* into its slider callbacks and a raw slider
  pointer into the arg's own signal (the sigc::bind calls in ArgTable::makeRow,
  src/gui/ArgTable.cpp:328). The patch bar's amplitude slider deliberately does
  neither -- it goes through the channel number and drops its subscriptions in
  populate() -- so the pattern to copy is already in the tree, next door.

- A Gtk-CRITICAL on reopening the Patch Selector:
  gtk_css_node_insert_after: assertion 'previous_sibling == NULL ||
  previous_sibling->parent == parent' failed.
  GTK recovers and the window is drawn correctly, but it is a real complaint
  about how the widget tree is being built.
  It does not reproduce on any single window. Opening the patch selector, the
  MIDI map or the keyboard alone is clean; so is opening two of them, and so is
  closing one and reopening it. The sequence that produces it is all three
  open, then close the patch selector and reopen it -- which points at a window
  being presented while others exist rather than at that window's own
  construction. Filling the selector's two grids before parenting them rather
  than after was the obvious candidate and did not stop it. What would settle
  it is a backtrace: G_DEBUG=fatal-criticals under a debugger, at the moment of
  the assertion.

- PATCH_PATH is defined and referenced nowhere. Set in CMakeLists.txt:366,
  emitted by cmake/config.h.in:44, used by no source file.

- gthMidiQueue::drain clears its notify flag before the pop loop rather than
  after. Clearing after leaves a roughly two-instruction window in which a push
  sees the flag still set, skips its emit(), and strands a message until the
  next one arrives -- live, a note that hangs. The ordering is argued in a
  comment, not tested.

- patches/pads/Rythmic.patch and Rythmic-2.patch both point at
  /usr/local/share//thinksynth/dsp/mfm03.dsp, which does not exist. These two
  are why the CI gates run over 99 of 101 patches; fixing them shrinks the
  exclusion filter by itself.


The corpus
----------

- 11 of the 92 shipped DSPs do not load at all. They reference input/wav,
  input/alsa or misc/wlan -- plugins that compile but are deliberately not in
  the build, wav.cpp's own description string being "Wav Input (BROKEN)".
  Remove or fix (argify) the obsolete ones.

- Four DSPs have numerically unstable filters and are being saved by the
  limiter rather than fixed: old/test.dsp diverges to 1.75e5, old/bd10.dsp to
  2e4. They want looking at separately.  (AUDIO.md)


Coverage
--------

- dspcheck should grow a "load this and process it" check for anything the
  editor writes.

- A dspstress TSan job is deliberately out of the default matrix: TSan cannot
  be combined with ASan so it needs its own build tree, and the run is slow.
  Worth a nightly schedule: trigger once the rest is stable.

- thSynthTree::buildSynthTree walks the graph recursively with a recalc flag
  rather than a proper topological order. An editor makes it easy to build
  pathological graphs.


Engine
------

- RT-safety. thSynth::process() still builds std::strings in its inner loop
  (argname = OUTPUTPREFIX; argname += ...), uses VLAs sized by window length,
  and inserts into std::map. The command queue bought race freedom, not RT
  purity; this wants the same plumbing underneath it.  (ARCHITECTURE.md)

- Slider values can be torn-read. Deliberate -- routing every drag through the
  queue would be silly -- but the honest fix is an atomic or a smoothed
  parameter.

- Arg metadata: a description, a sane range and a default per arg. regArg()
  already carries direction; the rest of that proposal is what would improve
  the parameter panel. MODULE_IFACE_VER is still 4 and ARG_IN defaults, so
  nothing needs a version bump.

- A @chanarg cannot be driven by a node. "This parameter varies with an LFO"
  works today; "the knob moves on its own" needs engine work.


Node editor
-----------

- A control's .min/.max/.label are read but not editable. Changing a control's
  range means rewriting three more lines -- the same splice applied three
  times.

- The palette's control dialog does not ask for a group. The writer already
  takes one.


Housekeeping
------------

- An untracked autotools-era config.h sits in the tree root ("Generated from
  config.h.in by configure"). It is in .gitignore, so it is invisible to git
  status while shadowing the generated one on any include path that reaches the
  source root. Delete it.

